CS 622 – Assignment 1: DonkeyPong

Author: Cole Johnson

Overview

This project is a conceptual object-oriented design demonstration based on the classic Donkey Kong game.
The goal of the assignment is not to build a fully functional game, but to define classes, methods,
and object interactions that demonstrate core object-oriented programming (OOP) principles discussed in class.
The program simulates gameplay behavior through printed console output, using a tick-based progression to represent time.

Objectives

-Class and object definitions
-Inheritance and polymorphism
-Method overriding
-Method overloading
-Encapsulation and separation of responsibilities
-Object collaboration through method calls
-Executable Java code with visible runtime output
-No graphics, real-time input, or full game loop are implemented, per assignment requirements.

Class Design Summary

GameObject (Base Class)

-Abstract base class for movable game entities
-Stores position (x, y)
-Declares an abstract update() method
-Provides shared collision logic

Mario

-Player-controlled character
-Has lives and position
-Supports movement, jumping (method overloading), climbing ladders, and taking damage
-Interacts with the map to determine ladder availability

DonkeyKong

-Enemy character
-Responsible for creating and throwing barrels
-Tracks the total number of barrels thrown
-Demonstrates method overriding through update()

Barrel

-Hazard object created by Donkey Kong
-Has damage and speed attributes
-Can collide with Mario and reduce lives
-Prints its behavior during each update tick

Map

-Represents the static game environment
-Stores map dimensions
-Owns all ladder objects
-Provides query methods for ladder access (getLadderAt)

Ladder

-Simple data object representing climbable structures
-Defined by position and height
-Does not inherit from GameObject since it has no active behavior

Direction
-Enumeration used to represent movement directions
-Improves readability and prevents invalid direction values

Tick-Based Simulation

The game is demonstrated through a series of manual ticks, where each tick represents a single moment in simulated game time.
During each tick:
-Active objects execute their update() method
-Key behaviors such as movement, barrel throwing, climbing, and collisions occur
-Output is printed to the console to show progression
This approach demonstrates time-based behavior without requiring a real game loop.

Output

Console output includes:
-Tick labels
-Movement messages
-Ladder climbing actions
-Barrel throws
-Collision detection
-Life reduction for Mario

How to Run
1. Open the project in a Java IDE
2. Ensure all .java files are in the same package or default package
3. Run the GameDemo class
4. Observe the console output for simulated gameplay progression

Please Note
-All movement and interactions are intentionally hardcoded to simplify demonstration
-No real user input or graphics are used